    "case_id": "474",
    "case_summary": "A 16-year-old male with chromosome 18q deletion and well-controlled epilepsy presented after four days of fever and one generalised seizure at home. Although he had no respiratory symptoms, his mother was ill with a cough. Patient's medical history included a normal echocardiogram from 2016 done for evaluation of a possible atrial septal defect, which showed no abnormal cardiac anatomy and normal cardiac function.\n\nThe patient presented with hemodynamic shock requiring:- Intubation- Intravenous crystalloid fluids (>40 mL/kg)- Intravenous epinephrine infusion (0.4 μg/kg per min)- Intravenous stress-dose hydrocortisone (100 mg)\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Troponin-I peaked at 32 ng/mL- N-terminal brain natriuretic peptide peaked at 9959 pg/mL (normal range <1384 pg/mL)- Low ADAMTS13 activity- Elevated von Willebrand factor activity- Normal fibrinogen concentration- Evidence of kidney injury, liver injury, and coagulopathyImaging Studies:- Chest radiographs showed bilateral hazy opacities- Peak oxygenation index of 4.8Cardiac Studies:- Serial electrocardiograms showed non-specific ST segment abnormalities- Echocardiogram findings:  - Moderately depressed biventricular function (ejection fraction 42%; normal range >55%)  - Moderate tricuspid valve regurgitationFigure 1 Title: Laboratory findings and medication trends in relation to therapeutic plasma exchangeTable 1 Title: Laboratory data at presentation",
